Part 1 - Identifying the Race Condition The bug you're encountering in the debounced auto-save flow of your JavaScript module can be traced back to a race condition. A race condition happens when two or more code paths compete for control, leading to inconsistencies or incorrect results. In this case, the root cause is due to an inefficient debounce implementation that doesn't properly handle simultaneous rapid keypress bursts. The debounced auto-save flow aims to limit how often a function gets called despite frequent user input changes. However, instead of debouncing the actual updates, it debounces the delay between each call of the autosave function itself. This means that if two keypresses happen within the debounce timeout interval, only one autosave will actually be triggered, leading to inconsistent data saves. Part 2 - Correcting the Race Condition To fix this issue, replace the existing debounce implementation with a more robust debouncing approach that correctly handles simultaneous user edits: ```javascript
let lastEditTime = null;
const debounceTimeout = null; function debounce(updateFunction) { return function(...args) { const currentTime = Date.now(); if (lastEditTime && currentTime - lastEditTime < 250) { // 250ms debounce delay updateFunction.apply(this, args); return; } lastEditTime = currentTime; debounceTimeout = setTimeout(() => updateFunction.apply(this, args), 250); };
}
``` This updated code snippet uses a 250ms debounce delay. If a new edit happens within this interval, the previous debounced call

